From 19f337313bcd828f1450f790367aa183de490a2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 20:17:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Web server: a deadline before login, kernel random numbers, a
 wider cross-site guard

- httpd serves one connection at a time, so a client that trickled a request held the interface
  for everybody. A connection now has 15 s to get past the login check (a kernel timer called by
  number; libshared has its own timer_create), none after login or on a router not set up yet. The
  stock ban list is no longer called.
- Every rand() in httpd reads the kernel's generator (--wrap=rand, reaper_rand.c).
- The phone-app second admin password (http_passwd_app) is kept empty, and the settings gate
  refuses one.
- The cross-site guard also covers the USB share, WAN action and safe-removal pages; another port
  on the router is another site, and Sec-Fetch-Site same-site counts as cross-site.
- The request being served is kept in a private file for the watchdog, not written to nvram before
  login.
- Gatekeeper's waiting-room page recognises approved devices again: it read the nvram copy of the
  device list, empty since v2.6.3.
- A settings import merges into the Gatekeeper list under its lock; a source login lock lasts its
  full time; the IPv6 remote-access listener honours -6; lan_gateway must be an IPv4 address
  (libwebapi).
Also in these files: web.c serves the Encrypted DNS page's status (both legs, the plain-DNS
devices, the DoH setup) and the remote log server counters, and carries the new Tweaks settings in
the settings file.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
